swehouse.c: closed-form cusp speeds for the nine interpolated systems

Sunshine (I, i), Pullen SD (L), Pullen SR (Q), Sripati (S), Meridian (X),
Morinus (M), Carter (F), Alcabitius (B) and APC (Y) differenced their cusps
against armc with two extra CalcH() calls. That works until a cusp turns
quickly, which is exactly where a chart wants the speed. Each now
differentiates its own formula and drops the two calls with it.

After this step no case block sets do_interpol. The machinery is kept, and is
raised only when an analytic speed or a cusp position comes back non-finite, so
an unexamined input degrades to the old behaviour rather than returning garbage.
Measured over 663,520 calls with speeds requested, it was raised 0 times.

At armc 270, geolat 66.5607089, eps 23.4392911, the eight systems that returned
up to 15,551,878 degrees per day now return at most 665. Across all
twenty six the largest there is 1,578, on Gauquelin, which was returning
331 at that point; Placidus also rises there, 331 to 596. Those are the
two letters the change makes larger, and both had been returning the
cheap frozen-pole value this series replaces.

Part of an eight-step series. Applied in order, the eight produce a file
byte-identical to the single patch "swehouse.c: compute house cusp speeds
analytically"; take either the series or that patch, not both. Every step
compiles. Steps 01 to 06 also apply to a clean tree on their own, so any one of
them can be taken without the rest; 07 needs 01 to 05, not 06; 08 needs all seven to link. Do not
drop 02 or 04 from the middle: nothing else needs them, so the result still
builds clean and is quietly wrong.
